--TEST--
`Core\Task::map` is bounded by the same `{limit, deadline}` shape as `Core\Task::all` and by no other spelling: `timeout` is still not an option, `limit` is still a `uint`, and there is no second member carrying a bound — `rule:concurrency/map-preserves-keys-and-order` and `rule:concurrency/limit-and-deadline-are-the-only-bounds`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 gives `all` and `map` one options shape between them, so the question
// worth asking of the second member is not what it accepts but whether it
// *agrees* with the first. A member that grew its own bounds would still look
// right read on its own line; it fails here.
//
// The three refusals are the three ways that agreement could have been lost.
// `timeout` is the name § 3 spends a paragraph refusing, and R2's trailing
// options shape is what leaves no room for it. `limit: "two"` asks whether the
// option's declared type survived being written a second time. And a
// `mapLimit` naming its bound in the member is the shape R20 forbids outright
// — one way to say one thing — which is why the absence is asserted rather
// than assumed.
class Probe {
    public static function bounds(): void {
        var $c = Core\Task::map([1], fn(int $n): int => $n, {timeout: 5s});
        var $d = Core\Task::map([1], fn(int $n): int => $n, {limit: "two"});
        Core\Task::mapLimit([1], fn(int $n): int => $n, 2);
        echo 1;
    }
}

Probe::bounds();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0454]: `timeout` is not an option of this member
   --> case.nvs:16:62
%A
error[E0401]: expected `uint`, found `string`
   --> case.nvs:17:69
%A
error[E0405]: `Core\Task` has no member named `mapLimit`
   --> case.nvs:18:9
%A
